#!/usr/bin/env srr-uvrun
"""SRR external ingest strategy: an X/Twitter account -> SRR items, read
through a Nitter instance (default: https://nitter.net).

Runs in the toolbox's shared uv project venv (repo-root pyproject.toml, via
the srr-uvrun shebang wrapper) like every bin/ script; needs beautifulsoup4
(description rewriting) and httpx[http2] (feed fetch + video resolution —
some instances 403 plain HTTP/1.1) from that venv.

X serves no feeds and its read API is paid, but nitter.net rebuilds a public
account's timeline as RSS. This strategy fetches that RSS and rewrites every
nitter artifact back to a canonical URL, so the STORED items never depend on
the instance staying alive (only the next fetch does):

  * proxied media  <instance>/pic/<encoded>  ->  https://pbs.twimg.com/...
    (the public Twitter CDN; /pic/orig/ becomes the ?name=orig full-size)
  * mentions, hashtags, statuses  <instance>/...  ->  https://x.com/...
    (nitter's #m fragments dropped); external links pass through untouched —
    except piped.video (nitter's replaceYouTube rewrite on some instances),
    which goes back to www.youtube.com / youtu.be
  * item links -> https://x.com/<author>/status/<id>
  * nitter's inline max-width styles stripped (the reader sizes images)

Nitter's title prefixes (per its rss.nimf, mutually exclusive):
  * "RT by @x: "  retweets/reposts — kept, prefix and all, and attributed to
    the original author with a leading "RT @author" body line; drop them with
    --no-retweets. Quote-reposts arrive as normal tweets with the quote as a
    blockquote.
  * "R to @x: "   replies — only self-thread continuations exist here (the
    profile RSS mirrors X's Posts tab, and nitter.net serves no
    with_replies RSS); stripped from the title, attributed with a leading
    "↩ replying to @x" body line; drop them with --no-replies.
  * "Pinned: "    the pinned tweet re-surfacing at the top — prefix stripped;
    guid dedup means it's ingested once, old pubDate and all.
Video tweets play in place by default: nitter's Video/GIF placeholder
becomes a real <video> element (see VIDEOS below; --no-videos keeps the old
clickable poster linking to the tweet, like srr-youtube's thumbnails). The
synthesized body survives #sanitize (img / a / video attrs are allowlisted;
no scripts). Tweets have no natural titles — the tweet text lands in the
title, capped like srr-telegram's, so consider srr's no-title feed option
(a media-only tweet falls back to "@user · date").

Subscribe with any form that names the account — an x.com / twitter.com /
nitter URL, @handle, or bare handle:
  srr feed add -t "NASA" -u "https://x.com/NASA" -i "srr-x"

THE INSTANCE — --instance
-------------------------
Fetches default to https://nitter.net. Nitter instances are mortal (X keeps
fighting them): when one dies, point the strategy elsewhere — one config
edit, nothing stored needs rewriting (the https:// scheme is optional):
  srr feed add -t "..." -u "https://x.com/NASA" \\
    -i "srr-x --instance https://nitter.poast.org"
Instance intel (all measured from this LAN, 2026-07-04):
  * nitter.net           RSS ok, but a harsh per-IP token bucket: absorbs a
                         burst, then HTTP 429 with ~40s/request refill while
                         its Retry-After claims 1s. Fine for a few feeds at
                         fetch-loop cadence; painful beyond that.
  * nitter.poast.org     RSS ok and burst-tolerant (5 rapid hits -> 200).
                         Its WAF 403s BROWSER UAs *and* all of HTTP/1.1 (the
                         reason srr-x fetches over HTTP/2), so don't panic
                         when it "fails" in Firefox or curl --http1.1.
  * rss.xcancel.com      xcancel's dedicated RSS host (xcancel.com/…/rss
                         redirects there; srr-x follows it), burst-tolerant,
                         but answers a whitelist-request stub until you email
                         them your reader ID (the stub tells you how).
  * everyone else tracked by status.d420.de had RSS disabled or broken.
Note nitter serves no ETag/Last-Modified today; the etag/last-modified
cursor is honored per the external-ingest contract anyway, so a future
instance that does send them gets 304s for free.

VIDEOS — default on, --no-videos to opt out
-------------------------------------------
Nitter's RSS carries only a poster thumbnail for a video tweet, and every
usable instance challenge-gates its HTML pages (measured 2026-07-05:
nitter.net answers empty 200s, poast/xcancel a browser-verification stub),
so the mp4 is resolved through X's own syndication CDN instead — the
unauthenticated cdn.syndication.twimg.com endpoint behind publish.twitter.com
embeds: one request per video tweet per changed cycle, none of it touching
the nitter instance. The highest-bitrate mp4 variant lands in a <video>
element with the pbs.twimg.com thumb as its poster — both canonical
Twitter-CDN URLs, so stored items stay instance-independent. GIF tweets skip
the lookup entirely: nitter ships them as a native <video> whose mp4 rides
its /pic/ proxy (captured 2026-07-05) — both URLs are rewritten canonical and
the element is rebuilt flat, because srr's #sanitize would otherwise drop the
<source> child (an unplayable poster-only box). GIFs keep GIF-style playback
(autoplay muted loop, allowed by srr's sanitizer since v3.3.2; older
backends strip the trio, degrading to click-to-play).
With --no-videos a GIF degrades to its canonical poster image. (A
placeholder-style GIF thumb would also resolve request-free: its mp4 key is
the thumb filename, tweet_video_thumb/K.jpg -> tweet_video/K.mp4.)
The endpoint is unofficial,
so any per-tweet failure (endpoint changed, deleted tweet, HLS-only
variants) degrades that item to the old clickable-poster placeholder and the
cycle continues; a transport error stops further lookups for the rest of the
run.

SELF-HOSTING MEDIA — --selfhost
-------------------------------
By default media stays hotlinked at its rewritten Twitter-CDN URLs
(pbs.twimg.com images, video.twimg.com mp4s) — nothing lands in the store.
Add --selfhost to download each image, video and poster into the SRR store
instead (straight from the CDN, never through the nitter proxy). A download
that fails (CDN hiccup, over srr's --max-asset-size cap — which actually
bites on video) falls back to its hotlink: the CDN URLs are public and
stable, so degrading beats failing the cycle. `srr preview` sends no
asset_dir (self-hosting off), so previews hotlink too.

MANUAL RUNS (parameters instead of stdin)
-----------------------------------------
Normally srr drives this script by writing a JSON request to stdin. To test
an account by hand, pass it as an argument instead — the request is built
from parameters and the result is pretty-printed:

  srr-x "https://x.com/NASA"
  srr-x --selfhost @NASA --asset-dir /tmp/store

  <account>                 the account: an x.com/twitter.com/nitter URL,
                            @handle, or bare handle
  --instance <url>          the nitter instance serving the RSS (default
                            https://nitter.net; scheme optional)
  --etag <value>            HTTP ETag from the previous run (sent as
                            If-None-Match; unchanged feed -> not_modified)
  --last-modified <value>   HTTP Last-Modified from the previous run
                            (sent as If-Modified-Since)
  --asset-dir <dir>         store dir to download media into (what srr
                            sends as asset_dir; only used with --selfhost)
  --max-asset-size <bytes>  per-file media cap (absent/0 = no cap)
"""
import sys, os, re, json, html, math, http.client
import urllib.parse, urllib.request, urllib.error
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET
from email.utils import parsedate_to_datetime

import httpx
from bs4 import BeautifulSoup

NS = {"dc": "http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"}

VERSION = "1.5"

UA = {"User-Agent": "SRR-x"}
MAX_FEED_BYTES = 8_000_000     # feed-XML read cap (the request carries no knob)
SELFHOST = False               # set by --selfhost; media downloads only when set
NO_RETWEETS = False            # set by --no-retweets; drops "RT by @..." items
NO_REPLIES = False             # set by --no-replies; drops "R to @..." items
VIDEOS = True                  # cleared by --no-videos; resolve video placeholders
INSTANCE = "https://nitter.net"  # set by --instance; who serves the RSS

USER_RE = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9_]{1,15}$")
# Nitter's reply title jargon (rss.nimf): "R to @<user>: <text>". Mutually
# exclusive with the "Pinned: " and "RT by @<user>: " prefixes, in that order.
REPLY_RE = re.compile(r"^R to (@[A-Za-z0-9_]{1,15}): (.*)$", re.S)
# A video placeholder in nitter's RSS is a status link wrapping a video-family
# thumbnail; the thumb path is the robust signal (rss.nimf's "Video"/"GIF"
# label text is not). GIF thumbs carry their mp4's key in the filename.
VIDEO_THUMB_RE = re.compile(r"^https://pbs\.twimg\.com/(?:amplify_video_thumb|"
                            r"ext_tw_video_thumb|tweet_video_thumb)/")
GIF_THUMB_RE = re.compile(
    r"^https://pbs\.twimg\.com/tweet_video_thumb/([A-Za-z0-9_-]+)\.")
STATUS_RE = re.compile(r"^https://x\.com/[^/]+/status/(\d+)$")
# A bare 11-char YouTube video id as the whole path — what a youtu.be short
# link's path looks like after nitter's replaceYouTube host swap.
YT_ID_RE = re.compile(r"^/[A-Za-z0-9_-]{11}$")


def fnv1a32(s: str) -> int:
    h = 0x811C9DC5
    for b in s.encode("utf-8"):
        h = ((h ^ b) * 0x01000193) & 0xFFFFFFFF
    return h


def log(*a):
    print("srr-x:", *a, file=sys.stderr)


def username_from_url(url: str) -> str:
    """Any accepted account form -> bare username (or a loud exit)."""
    u = (url or "").strip().lstrip("@")
    if "/" in u:                       # URL form: the first path segment
        if "://" not in u:
            u = "https://" + u
        path = urllib.parse.urlsplit(u).path.strip("/")
        u = path.split("/", 1)[0].lstrip("@")
    if not USER_RE.match(u):
        sys.exit(f"srr-x: cannot find an X username in {url!r}")
    return u


def feed_url(url: str) -> str:
    return f"{INSTANCE}/{username_from_url(url)}/rss"


# The feed fetch rides httpx over HTTP/2: nitter.poast.org's WAF 403s every
# HTTP/1.1 request (measured 2026-07-04 — same URL and UA pass on h2), and
# urllib can't speak h2. CDN media downloads stay on urllib below: pbs.twimg.com
# has no such gate, and download()'s semantics are shared with srr-youtube.
HTTP = None


def http_client():
    global HTTP
    if HTTP is None:
        HTTP = httpx.Client(http2=True, headers=UA, follow_redirects=True,
                            timeout=30)
    return HTTP


def fetch_feed(req):
    """GET the account's RSS off the instance honoring the etag/last-modified
    cursor. -> (body, etag, last_modified), or None when nothing changed (304)."""
    hdr = {}
    if req.get("etag"):
        hdr["If-None-Match"] = req["etag"]
    if req.get("last_modified"):
        hdr["If-Modified-Since"] = req["last_modified"]
    cap = MAX_FEED_BYTES
    with http_client().stream("GET", feed_url(req["url"]), headers=hdr) as r:
        if r.status_code == 304:          # nothing new since our cursor
            return None
        if r.status_code == 429:
            # Retrying in-process is pointless: nitter.net's Retry-After says
            # 1s but the drained bucket measured ~40s/request (2026-07-04).
            sys.exit(f"srr-x: {urllib.parse.urlsplit(INSTANCE).netloc} "
                     f"rate-limited this IP (HTTP 429; its Retry-After header "
                     f"is untrustworthy) — wait it out, space the polls, or "
                     f"switch --instance (see --help)")
        r.raise_for_status()
        body = b""
        for chunk in r.iter_bytes(64 * 1024):
            body += chunk
            if len(body) >= cap:
                break
    return (body[:cap], r.headers.get("ETag", ""),
            r.headers.get("Last-Modified", ""))


def direct_media_url(enc_path: str) -> str:
    """nitter's /pic/<percent-encoded> proxy path -> direct CDN URL. Host-less
    paths (media/..., amplify_video_thumb/...) live on pbs.twimg.com; a leading
    orig/ is nitter's full-size marker, ?name=orig on the CDN."""
    p = urllib.parse.unquote(enc_path)
    size = ""
    if p.startswith("orig/"):
        p, size = p[len("orig/"):], "?name=orig"
    if "." not in p.split("/", 1)[0]:     # first segment isn't a hostname
        p = "pbs.twimg.com/" + p
    return "https://" + p + size


def rewrite_url(u: str, host: str) -> str:
    """Point a nitter URL back at its canonical home; leave the rest alone."""
    parts = urllib.parse.urlsplit(u)
    if parts.netloc == "piped.video":
        # Some instances set nitter's replaceYouTube=piped.video, which
        # host-swaps every youtube.com/youtu.be link in the tweet text for
        # that third-party frontend (as mortal as nitter itself). Exact
        # inverse: a bare video-id path is a youtu.be short link, anything
        # else (watch/shorts/channel/@handle) lives on www.youtube.com.
        yt = "youtu.be" if YT_ID_RE.match(parts.path) else "www.youtube.com"
        return urllib.parse.urlunsplit(("https", yt, parts.path, parts.query, ""))
    if parts.netloc != host:
        return u
    if parts.path.startswith("/pic/"):
        return direct_media_url(parts.path[len("/pic/"):])
    # mention / status / search: same path+query on x.com, #m fragment dropped
    return urllib.parse.urlunsplit(("https", "x.com", parts.path, parts.query, ""))


# WHERE VIDEO URLS COME FROM: nitter's RSS carries only a poster thumbnail for
# a video tweet, and every usable instance challenge-gates its HTML pages
# (measured 2026-07-05: nitter.net answers empty 200s, poast/xcancel a
# browser-verification stub), so tweet pages can't be scraped for the mp4.
# X's own syndication CDN — the unauthenticated endpoint behind
# publish.twitter.com embeds — still resolves a tweet id to its
# video.twimg.com mp4 variants. It's unofficial, so every failure degrades to
# the clickable-poster placeholder instead of failing the cycle.
SYNDICATION = "https://cdn.syndication.twimg.com/tweet-result"

SYND_CACHE = {}                # tweet id -> syndication JSON (or None), per run

# Like `probing` for media downloads: the first syndication transport error
# latches resolution off for the rest of the run.
resolving = True


def syndication_token(tid: str) -> str:
    """The token X's embed widget derives from the tweet id, ported from its
    JS: ((id/1e15)*PI).toString(36).replace(/(0+|.)/g, ''). Measured
    2026-07-05: the value isn't actually validated (any non-empty token
    works), only its absence is — sending the real derivation anyway keeps
    the request indistinguishable from the widget's."""
    x = (int(tid) / 1e15) * math.pi
    digits = "0123456789abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz"
    n, ipart = int(x), ""
    while n:
        n, r = divmod(n, 36)
        ipart = digits[r] + ipart
    frac, fpart = x - int(x), ""
    for _ in range(10):        # past 10 base-36 digits a float64 is noise
        frac *= 36
        fpart += digits[int(frac)]
        frac -= int(frac)
    return (ipart + "." + fpart).replace("0", "").replace(".", "")


def syndication_lookup(tid: str):
    """One tweet's syndication record, memoized per run; None = unresolvable."""
    global resolving
    if tid in SYND_CACHE:
        return SYND_CACHE[tid]
    data = None
    if resolving:
        try:
            r = http_client().get(SYNDICATION, params={
                "id": tid, "token": syndication_token(tid)})
            if r.status_code == 200:
                data = r.json()
            else:
                log(f"syndication HTTP {r.status_code} for tweet {tid}")
        except httpx.HTTPError as e:
            resolving = False  # network trouble; stop asking this run
            log(f"syndication unreachable, keeping placeholders: {e}")
        except ValueError as e:
            log(f"syndication sent no JSON for tweet {tid}: {e}")
    SYND_CACHE[tid] = data
    return data


def video_url(tid: str, thumb: str):
    """The canonical mp4 behind a video placeholder, or None to keep the
    placeholder. GIF thumbs carry their mp4 key (request-free); real videos
    ask the syndication CDN and take the highest-bitrate mp4 variant (m3u8
    variants are useless here: browsers don't play bare HLS in <video>)."""
    m = GIF_THUMB_RE.match(thumb)
    if m:
        return f"https://video.twimg.com/tweet_video/{m.group(1)}.mp4"
    medias = [md for md in (syndication_lookup(tid) or {}).get("mediaDetails")
              or [] if md.get("video_info")]
    match = [md for md in medias if md.get("media_url_https") == thumb]
    if not match:
        if len(medias) != 1:   # thumb drifted and it's ambiguous: give up
            return None
        match = medias
    mp4s = [v for v in match[0]["video_info"].get("variants") or []
            if v.get("content_type") == "video/mp4" and v.get("url")]
    return max(mp4s, key=lambda v: v.get("bitrate") or 0)["url"] if mp4s else None


def download(url: str, dest: str, cap: int) -> str:
    """Stream url to dest honoring the byte cap; a partial file never lands at
    dest (.part + rename, so a run killed mid-download can't leave a truncated
    file for a later cycle to reuse). -> 'ok' | 'toobig' | 'missing' | 'error'."""
    tmp = dest + ".part"
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(
                urllib.request.Request(url, headers=UA), timeout=30) as r:
            clen = int(r.headers.get("Content-Length") or 0)
            if cap and clen > cap:
                return "toobig"
            os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(dest), exist_ok=True)
            n = 0
            with open(tmp, "wb") as fh:
                while True:
                    chunk = r.read(256 * 1024)
                    if not chunk:
                        break
                    n += len(chunk)
                    if cap and n > cap:
                        return "toobig"
                    fh.write(chunk)
        if n == 0 or (clen and n != clen):
            return "error"    # empty or truncated body
        os.replace(tmp, dest)
        return "ok"
    except urllib.error.HTTPError as e:
        return "missing" if e.code == 404 else "error"
    except (OSError, ValueError, http.client.HTTPException):
        # HTTPException covers IncompleteRead: a server closing early with a
        # Content-Length set raises it mid-read, and it is NOT an OSError.
        return "error"
    finally:
        if os.path.exists(tmp):
            try:
                os.remove(tmp)
            except OSError:
                pass


def asset_rel(url: str) -> str:
    """Store-relative name for a CDN URL: content-addressed by the URL itself
    (Twitter media keys are opaque; hashing dodges cross-directory collisions)."""
    ext = os.path.splitext(urllib.parse.urlsplit(url).path)[1] or ".jpg"
    return "x/%08x%s" % (fnv1a32(url), ext)


# A failed download other than a plain 404 latches downloading off for the rest
# of the run, so a CDN hiccup degrades to hotlinks once instead of stalling on
# every image.
probing = True


def selfhost_media(url: str, asset_dir: str, cap: int):
    """Download one image into the store. -> '#/x/<name>' marker, or None to
    fall back to hotlinking (missing media, over-cap file, CDN trouble)."""
    global probing
    rel = asset_rel(url)
    dest = os.path.join(asset_dir, rel)
    if os.path.exists(dest) and os.path.getsize(dest) > 0:
        # Refresh the mtime so srr's post-cycle age-based cache sweep never
        # deletes a file a live feed still consumes.
        os.utime(dest, None)
        return "#/" + rel
    if not probing:
        return None
    st = download(url, dest, cap)
    if st == "ok":
        return "#/" + rel
    if st == "toobig":
        log(f"media over asset cap {cap}: {url}")
    elif st == "error":
        probing = False       # network trouble, not a missing file
    return None


def flat_video(soup, src, poster, gif):
    """The one <video> shape emitted everywhere: the attr set srr's #sanitize
    allowlists, same as srr-telegram's. GIFs additionally autoplay muted in a
    loop — how every platform renders them; autoplay is only honored by
    browsers when muted, so the trio travels together (needs the deployed srr
    sanitizer >= v3.3.2 — older ones strip it, degrading to click-to-play)."""
    v = soup.new_tag("video", src=src)
    if poster:
        v["poster"] = poster
    v["controls"] = ""
    v["preload"] = "metadata"
    v["playsinline"] = ""
    if gif:
        v["autoplay"] = v["muted"] = v["loop"] = ""
    return v


def build_content(desc_html, host, creator, is_rt, reply_to, asset_dir, cap):
    """Rewrite one item's description: every URL pointed home, styles dropped,
    --selfhost markers swapped in, retweets attributed to their author and
    replies to the account they answer."""
    soup = BeautifulSoup(desc_html, "html.parser")
    for a in soup.find_all("a", href=True):
        old, new = a["href"], rewrite_url(a["href"], host)
        a["href"] = new
        # Quote citations show the raw nitter URL as the link text — keep the
        # visible text canonical too. Nitter's replaceYouTube swap also mangles
        # the (possibly truncated) display text; the rewritten href knows
        # which YouTube host it reversed to.
        if a.string:
            text = a.string.strip()
            if text == old:
                a.string.replace_with(new)
            elif text.startswith(("piped.video/", "https://piped.video/")):
                a.string.replace_with(text.replace(
                    "piped.video", urllib.parse.urlsplit(new).netloc, 1))
    # GIF tweets arrive as a native <video> — poster + /pic/-proxied mp4
    # <source> (captured 2026-07-05) — not a placeholder. Runs in BOTH modes:
    # stored URLs must never depend on the instance, and srr's #sanitize drops
    # <source> children (leaving an unplayable poster-only box) and strips
    # autoplay/muted/loop anyway, so the element is rebuilt flat with the
    # allowlisted attr set. Without --videos (or with no playable src) it
    # degrades to its canonical poster <img>, which the img pass below then
    # treats like any other image (incl. --selfhost).
    for v in soup.find_all("video"):
        el = v.find("source", src=True)
        raw = (el["src"] if el else v.get("src")) or ""
        src = rewrite_url(raw, host) if raw else ""
        poster = rewrite_url(v["poster"], host) if v.get("poster") else ""
        if VIDEOS and src.startswith("https://"):
            gif = "/tweet_video/" in src      # decide before markers hide it
            if SELFHOST and asset_dir:
                if poster:
                    poster = selfhost_media(poster, asset_dir, cap) or poster
                src = selfhost_media(src, asset_dir, cap) or src
            v.replace_with(flat_video(soup, src, poster, gif))
        elif poster:
            v.replace_with(soup.new_tag("img", src=poster))
        else:
            v.decompose()     # nothing renderable would survive #sanitize
    if VIDEOS:
        # Video placeholders (status link wrapping a video thumb) become real
        # <video> elements — the attr set srr's #sanitize allowlists, same as
        # srr-telegram's. Runs before the <img> pass: an unresolved
        # placeholder falls through to it and stays a clickable poster.
        for a in soup.find_all("a", href=True):
            tid = STATUS_RE.match(a["href"])
            img = a.find("img", src=True)
            if not (tid and img):
                continue
            thumb = rewrite_url(img["src"], host)
            if not VIDEO_THUMB_RE.match(thumb):
                continue
            src = video_url(tid.group(1), thumb)
            if not src:
                continue
            gif = "/tweet_video/" in src      # decide before markers hide it
            poster = thumb
            if SELFHOST and asset_dir:
                poster = selfhost_media(thumb, asset_dir, cap) or thumb
                src = selfhost_media(src, asset_dir, cap) or src
            a.replace_with(flat_video(soup, src, poster, gif))
    for img in soup.find_all("img", src=True):
        src = rewrite_url(img["src"], host)
        if SELFHOST and asset_dir and src.startswith("https://"):
            marker = selfhost_media(src, asset_dir, cap)
            if marker:
                src = marker
        attrs = {"src": src}              # drops nitter's max-width style
        if img.get("alt"):
            attrs["alt"] = img["alt"]
        img.attrs = attrs
    out = str(soup)
    if is_rt and creator:
        u = html.escape(creator.lstrip("@"))
        out = f'<p>RT <a href="https://x.com/{u}">@{u}</a></p>' + out
    elif reply_to:
        u = html.escape(reply_to.lstrip("@"))
        out = f'<p>↩ replying to <a href="https://x.com/{u}">@{u}</a></p>' + out
    return out


def build_items(body, asset_dir, cap):
    host = urllib.parse.urlsplit(INSTANCE).netloc
    SYND_CACHE.clear()         # the memo is per run, not per process
    items = []
    # lstrip: rss.xcancel.com pads its XML with leading whitespace, which the
    # XML spec (and ET) reject before the declaration.
    for it in ET.fromstring(body.lstrip()).iter("item"):
        title = (it.findtext("title") or "").strip()
        is_rt, reply_to = False, None
        if title.startswith("Pinned: "):      # old tweet re-surfacing at the
            title = title[len("Pinned: "):]   # top; guid dedup ingests it once
        elif title.startswith("RT by @"):
            is_rt = True
        else:
            m = REPLY_RE.match(title)
            if m:                             # a self-thread continuation (the
                reply_to, title = m.groups()  # Posts tab carries no other kind)
        if (NO_RETWEETS and is_rt) or (NO_REPLIES and reply_to):
            continue
        tid = (it.findtext("guid") or "").strip()
        link = (it.findtext("link") or "").strip()
        creator = (it.findtext("dc:creator", "", NS) or "").strip()
        try:
            pub = parsedate_to_datetime(it.findtext("pubDate", "")).isoformat()
        except (TypeError, ValueError):
            pub = None
        if not title:              # a media-only tweet has no text to title:
            title = " · ".join(   # fall back like srr-telegram's make_title
                filter(None, [creator, (pub or "")[:10]]))
        items.append({
            "guid": fnv1a32(tid or link),     # tweet ids are globally unique
            "title": (title[:117] + "…") if len(title) > 118 else title,
            "content": build_content(it.findtext("description") or "", host,
                                     creator, is_rt, reply_to, asset_dir, cap),
            "link": rewrite_url(link, host),
            "published": pub,
        })
    return items


def run_ingest(req):
    got = fetch_feed(req)
    if got is None:
        return {"not_modified": True}
    body, etag, last_modified = got
    asset_dir = req.get("asset_dir") or ""
    # srr's --max-asset-size cap, sent per request as max_asset_size (bytes);
    # 0 = no cap. Honored at download time, per the external-ingest contract.
    cap = int(req.get("max_asset_size") or 0)
    return {
        "items": build_items(body, asset_dir, cap),
        "etag": etag,
        "last_modified": last_modified,
    }


def strip_mode_flags(argv):
    """Pull the mode flags (valid in both stdin and manual runs) out of argv;
    what remains decides stdin vs manual mode."""
    global SELFHOST, NO_RETWEETS, NO_REPLIES, VIDEOS, INSTANCE
    out, i = [], 0
    while i < len(argv):
        a = argv[i]
        if a == "--selfhost":
            SELFHOST = True
        elif a == "--no-retweets":
            NO_RETWEETS = True
        elif a == "--no-replies":
            NO_REPLIES = True
        elif a == "--no-videos":
            VIDEOS = False
        elif a == "--instance":
            if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                sys.exit("srr-x: --instance needs a value")
            inst = argv[i + 1].rstrip("/")
            if "://" not in inst:  # same normalization username_from_url does;
                inst = "https://" + inst   # a scheme-less host would otherwise
            INSTANCE = inst                # break both the fetch and rewriting
            i += 1
        else:
            out.append(a)
        i += 1
    return out


CLI_PARAMS = {"--etag": "etag", "--last-modified": "last_modified",
              "--asset-dir": "asset_dir", "--max-asset-size": "max_asset_size"}


def request_from_argv(argv):
    """CLI parameters -> the same request dict srr sends as JSON on stdin."""
    req, i = {}, 0
    while i < len(argv):
        a = argv[i]
        if a in CLI_PARAMS:
            if i + 1 >= len(argv):
                sys.exit(f"srr-x: {a} needs a value")
            req[CLI_PARAMS[a]] = argv[i + 1]
            i += 2
        elif a.startswith("-"):
            sys.exit(f"srr-x: unknown option {a!r} (see --help)")
        elif "url" in req:
            sys.exit(f"srr-x: more than one account given "
                     f"({req['url']!r}, {a!r})")
        else:
            req["url"] = a
            i += 1
    if "url" not in req:
        sys.exit("srr-x: missing account (see --help)")
    if "max_asset_size" in req:
        try:
            req["max_asset_size"] = int(req["max_asset_size"])
        except ValueError:
            sys.exit(f"srr-x: --max-asset-size must be a byte count, "
                     f"got {req['max_asset_size']!r}")
    return req


def cmd_ingest(req=None):
    # req is built from CLI parameters (manual run) or, when None, read as the
    # JSON request srr writes to stdin (the SRR external-ingest contract).
    pretty = req is not None
    if req is None:
        try:
            req = json.load(sys.stdin)
        except json.JSONDecodeError as e:
            sys.exit(f"srr-x: could not parse request on stdin: {e}")
    try:
        result = run_ingest(req)
    except (httpx.HTTPError, urllib.error.URLError) as e:
        sys.exit(f"srr-x: feed fetch failed: {e}")
    except ET.ParseError as e:
        sys.exit(f"srr-x: feed is not valid XML: {e}")
    if pretty:
        # manual run: readable for a human, not byte-compatible with srr's
        json.dump(result, sys.stdout, indent=2, ensure_ascii=False)
        print()
    else:
        json.dump(result, sys.stdout)


def main():
    argv = strip_mode_flags(sys.argv[1:])
    if argv and argv[0] in ("-h", "--help", "help"):
        print(__doc__)
        return
    if argv and argv[0] in ("version", "--version", "-V"):
        print("srr-x", VERSION)
        return
    cmd_ingest(request_from_argv(argv) if argv else None)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()
